ven animals have gone  extinct in the u.s. three of them were  small freshwater fish one was a  freshwater clam and two were very small  birds  it's sad that they are gone but this is  not a mass extinction the number three  environmental disaster that didn't  happen the energy crisis on the first  Earth Day in 1970 ecologist Kenneth watt  declared by the year 2000 if present  trends continue we will be using up  crude oil at such a rate that there  won't be any more crude oil the energy  crisis erupted in the 1970s after the  Arab oil embargo against the United  States OPEC the organization of all  producing come from countries organized  the cartel and that cartel reduced  output from an output of a good is  reduced and demand stays the same  prices rise and of course what happened  is our government did all kinds of  idiotic things under President Richard  Nixon we started rationing gasoline  among other things the reality is that  every time the these resources become  more scarce the price goes up and in  entrepreneurs identify new ways of  supplying us with energy the number two  environmental disaster that didn't  happen a Silent Spring we've already  mentioned the importance of Rachel  Carson's book to the environmental  movement but Silent Spring wouldn't have  grabbed the number two slot in our  countdown if Carson's predictions had  come true this book was arguing that the  use of synthetic chemicals and  specifically pesticides was destroying  the natural world that the spring would  be silent because the birds would all be  poisoned by our pesticides ever since  well people were worried about  insecticides because insecticides kill  things she also was arguing that the use  of these synthetic chemicals were going  to create epidemics of cancer so it was  a really apocalyptic vision that was  frankly based on a flimsy and inaccurate  representation of marginal science  pesticides are not a big deal in fact a  cup of coffee has more carcinogens in it  then you're likely to get from exposure  to synthetic carcinogens getting here in  response to all the hype about  pesticides governments around the world  have banned DDT and those bans have led  to some decidedly unpleasant unintended  consequences DDT was the most effective  chemical for controlling malaria  mosquitoes around the world the result  well now a million people a year are  dying of malaria there is a cost-benefit  analysis here do we want to save Eagles  we want to say people I think we can say  both but the fact of the matter is is  that the band was too harsh too strong  minces a serious human tragedy that can  be directly correlated to Silent Spring  and now the number one environmental  disaster that didn't happen Malthusian  famine in his 1968 book the Population  Bomb Paul Ehrlich wrote the battle to  feed all of humanity is over in the  1970s hundreds of millions of people are  going to starve to death in spite of any  crash programs embarked upon now we  couldn't save countries like China we  couldn't save countries like India we  couldn't save a whole bunch of places in  Africa and what we had to do in order to  to forestall this overpopulation trend  is to stop sending them food Ehrlich was  just spectacularly wrong basically if we  had followed his policies we would have  actually created the famines that he  claimed were inevitable  although population has increased  considerably food output per capita has  risen faster what has happened is is  that when Erik was riding it around like  seventy the average woman on planet  earth was having about five children  over the course of her life that is down  to two point four now and continues to  fall unlike animals what we do is have  fewer children and invest more money in  them and make them better off more  people are being fed better today than  at any time in human history
 

